45
00:03:08,053 --> 00:03:14,260
Quick quiz. A stage file reaches the right
result with one hypothesis, confirmed,

46
00:03:14,300 --> 00:03:20,353
and a log written up afterwards. Is that
A, level 2, B, level 3, or C, level 4?

47
00:03:20,393 --> 00:03:21,279
Your answer?

48
00:03:26,119 --> 00:03:29,608
What separates level 2 from
level 3, in every dimension.

49
00:03:29,648 --> 00:03:34,902
The answer is A. At level 2 the result is
right and something is asserted that isn't

50
00:03:34,942 --> 00:03:40,133
shown. At level 3 it's on the page. The
evaluation's wording for debugging at level

51
00:03:40,173 --> 00:03:45,111
2 is exact: one hypothesis, confirmed.
The briefing's claims neither tested nor

52
00:03:45,151 --> 00:03:47,947
challenged. The log
reconstructed afterwards.

53
00:03:48,287 --> 00:03:51,942
What level 4 looks like. The
write-up predicts before it runs.

54
00:03:51,982 --> 00:03:55,159
It gives a second line of
evidence for its conclusion.

55
00:03:55,199 --> 00:03:58,436
It argues trade-offs on both
sides where they are real.

56
00:03:58,476 --> 00:04:03,322
It states the limits: what a control would
not have caught, what an instrument can

57
00:04:03,362 --> 00:04:06,838
and cannot restore. And it turns
the result into a control.

58
00:04:07,178 --> 00:04:11,738
The final judgment. "Demonstrates
senior-level mastery" requires all of the

59
00:04:11,778 --> 00:04:16,092
following. All eight checks pass, on
a sandbox worked through in order.

60
00:04:16,132 --> 00:04:21,183
No disqualifying finding. Every dimension
score is 3 or higher, where the dimension

61
00:04:21,223 --> 00:04:24,495
score is the median of its
stage levels, rounded down.

62
00:04:24,535 --> 00:04:28,420
The median is the middle value
when the levels are put in order.

63
00:04:28,760 --> 00:04:33,203
Every floor is 3 or higher, where the
floor is the lowest level among the

64
00:04:33,243 --> 00:04:38,178
dimension's primary stages: a senior
engineer doesn't have a category of incident

65
00:04:38,218 --> 00:04:43,275
that goes badly. At least three dimension
scores are 4, and they include production

66
00:04:43,315 --> 00:04:45,117
judgment or debugging ability.

67
00:04:45,457 --> 00:04:49,667
And the postmortem is at level 3 or
higher and has its closing section.

68
00:04:50,007 --> 00:04:54,451
Otherwise the outcome is "not yet", and
it comes with a list: each condition that

69
00:04:54,491 --> 00:04:58,824
failed, the stage and item where the
evidence was missing, and what to restudy.

70
00:04:59,164 --> 00:05:03,907
How to score yourself. The evaluation
allows self-evaluation under one condition:

71
00:05:03,947 --> 00:05:08,572
you score a day after finishing, from
what is on the page and not from what you

72
00:05:08,612 --> 00:05:13,415
remember meaning. If the evidence for a
level isn't written down, the level wasn't

73
00:05:13,455 --> 00:05:16,308
demonstrated, however
likely it is that you knew.

74
00:05:16,648 --> 00:05:21,523
The quickest read. The evaluator's notes
say where to look first in a stage file:

75
00:05:21,563 --> 00:05:25,952
the "Not verified" list, the options
table, and the number of hypotheses.

76
00:05:25,992 --> 00:05:28,318
Their absence is almost always level 2.

77
00:05:30,158 --> 00:05:34,824
A picture helps. Think of yourself as the
reviewer of a colleague's incident report,

78
00:05:34,864 --> 00:05:39,418
where the colleague happens to be you, a
week ago. A reviewer can't ask the author

79
00:05:39,458 --> 00:05:41,771
what they meant. The page is all there is.

80
00:05:42,111 --> 00:05:46,980
That isn't an artificial rule. It's exactly
the position of the CTO who reads your

81
00:05:47,020 --> 00:05:51,948
summary, of the auditor who reads your
postmortem a year later, and of the engineer

82
00:05:51,988 --> 00:05:56,916
who inherits the repository and wonders why
a branch was force-pushed on a Tuesday.

83
00:05:57,256 --> 00:06:00,304
Where the model breaks: a
real reviewer has no stake.

84
00:06:00,344 --> 00:06:04,849
You do, and the pull is always upward:
"I knew that, I did not write it down."

85
00:06:04,889 --> 00:06:08,170
The scale has an answer for
that sentence. It is level 2.

86
00:06:08,510 --> 00:06:13,118
The second model is for the comparison
with the replay: two climbers on different

87
00:06:13,158 --> 00:06:17,249
routes up the same face. You don't
ask whether they used the same holds.

88
00:06:17,289 --> 00:06:20,692
You ask whether each was roped
in before every exposed move.

89
00:06:20,732 --> 00:06:25,340
In a log, the rope is an anchor before a
rewrite, a lease on a forced push, which

90
00:06:25,380 --> 00:06:29,930
names the value the push expects on the
server, and a read-only phase before the

91
00:06:29,970 --> 00:06:30,676
first change.

92
00:06:32,516 --> 00:06:37,746
Read the sheet by columns before you fill
it in. Try it now, with the sheet on

93
00:06:37,786 --> 00:06:41,934
screen. Thirty seconds. Find
stage 3 and stage 7 in every row.

94
00:06:41,974 --> 00:06:47,069
For each, count how many dimensions it
feeds, and for how many it's primary.

95
00:06:47,109 --> 00:06:48,758
Say the numbers out loud.

96
00:06:53,598 --> 00:06:57,141
Stage 3 feeds six dimensions
and is primary for two.

97
00:06:57,181 --> 00:07:02,378
Stage 7 feeds all seven and is primary
for two. A weak stage 3 or 7 shows up

98
00:07:02,418 --> 00:07:07,822
everywhere. And read the last column: the
floor is why one bad day in a primary

99
00:07:07,862 --> 00:07:09,614
stage decides the outcome.

100
00:07:11,454 --> 00:07:16,492
Into the lab, eight times. Before each
replay, score that stage on your own sheet.

101
00:07:16,532 --> 00:07:20,579
Do stage 1 now, and say your level
for debugging ability out loud.

102
00:07:25,419 --> 00:07:30,621
Stage 1, a bug in production. Primary for
debugging ability and production judgment.

103
00:07:30,661 --> 00:07:34,491
The briefing came with a suspect
and with a ready-made revert.

104
00:07:34,531 --> 00:07:38,297
The model's third step tests the
suspect before believing it.

105
00:07:38,637 --> 00:07:43,352
With the proposed revert checked out, the
reproduction still routes the message to

106
00:07:43,392 --> 00:07:47,991
the wrong queue. The hypothesis of the
engineer on call is refuted with one run.

107
00:07:48,031 --> 00:07:52,282
Then a bisect, Git's binary search over
commits, between the two releases:

108
00:07:52,622 --> 00:07:56,516
The first bad commit is
5ce3510, another pull request.

109
00:07:56,856 --> 00:08:01,559
Look in your own log for two things. Did
you prove your reproduction on both known

110
00:08:01,599 --> 00:08:05,261
ends, the good release and the
bad one, before you relied on it?

111
00:08:05,301 --> 00:08:08,558
And did you test the claim in
the briefing, or act on it?

112
00:08:08,598 --> 00:08:13,070
Merging the speculative revert is one of
the shortcuts the evaluation lists as

113
00:08:13,110 --> 00:08:13,880
disqualifying.

114
00:08:14,220 --> 00:08:17,438
Stage 2, a merge conflict.
Primary for Git knowledge.

115
00:08:17,478 --> 00:08:21,986
A colleague had resolved "your conflicts"
and the pull request said clean.

116
00:08:22,326 --> 00:08:25,894
git show --remerge-diff on the
merge that the colleague made.

117
00:08:25,934 --> 00:08:30,625
That option repeats the merge and shows
what the person who resolved it changed.

118
00:08:30,665 --> 00:08:35,475
Both conflict regions were resolved for
one side, and the lines of your own change

119
00:08:35,515 --> 00:08:36,717
are the ones removed.

120
00:08:37,057 --> 00:08:41,965
You've seen this shape in Incident 9. In
your log: how did you find out what the

121
00:08:42,005 --> 00:08:46,788
merge did? A level 3 answer shows the
state, with this command or another that

122
00:08:46,828 --> 00:08:51,798
proves the same thing. A level 2 answer
says "the merge was wrong" and redoes it.

123
00:08:52,138 --> 00:08:57,040
Stage 3, a leaked secret. Primary for
security awareness and engineering

124
00:08:57,080 --> 00:08:58,001
communication.

125
00:08:58,341 --> 00:09:03,109
The tip has no such file. The history under
the tip has, and the pull request ref

126
00:09:03,149 --> 00:09:06,907
keeps the original commits: a
squash merge wouldn't change that.

127
00:09:06,947 --> 00:09:11,537
In your stage file, check the order first:
is the first action the revocation,

128
00:09:11,577 --> 00:09:14,326
stated with its reason,
before any Git command?

129
00:09:14,666 --> 00:09:19,451
Then the scope: did you list every ref in
every repository that reached the file,

130
00:09:19,491 --> 00:09:23,859
including the pull request ref and
anything else that named those commits?

131
00:09:23,899 --> 00:09:28,327
And one more line of the evaluation:
the dummy secret isn't copied into the

132
00:09:28,367 --> 00:09:29,101
deliverables.

133
00:09:29,441 --> 00:09:33,703
Stage 4, failed CI. Primary for GitHub
knowledge and debugging ability.

134
00:09:33,743 --> 00:09:37,156
"It passes on my machine; it
must be the Python version."

135
00:09:37,496 --> 00:09:41,800
Which commit did the run test? The pull
request has two refs, and they hold

136
00:09:41,840 --> 00:09:46,493
different IDs: the head, and the test
merge, the commit that merges the head into

137
00:09:46,533 --> 00:09:47,477
the current base.

138
00:09:47,817 --> 00:09:51,023
The model fetches the merge
ref and runs the tests on it.

139
00:09:51,063 --> 00:09:55,692
They fail locally, with a message that
names a symbol another pull request changed

140
00:09:55,732 --> 00:09:56,148
on main.

141
00:09:56,488 --> 00:10:00,922
Both statements of the briefing were
true: the head passes, the merge doesn't.

142
00:10:00,962 --> 00:10:05,740
In your log: did you answer the first
questions of the investigation order on paper,

143
00:10:05,780 --> 00:10:09,640
workflow, event, commit checked out,
before you reproduced anything?

144
00:10:09,680 --> 00:10:14,229
And in your "Not verified" list: did you
write what a local reproduction doesn't

145
00:10:14,269 --> 00:10:14,573
prove?

146
00:10:14,913 --> 00:10:19,777
Stage 5, lost work. Primary for
Git knowledge and recovery skills.

147
00:10:20,117 --> 00:10:24,832
This is the model stating what is gone: an
unstaged edit and a file that was never

148
00:10:24,872 --> 00:10:28,543
added. Git has no object for
either, and three commands show it.

149
00:10:28,583 --> 00:10:31,500
The stage's deliverable
asks for a two-column list.

150
00:10:31,540 --> 00:10:36,371
Recovered, with the mechanism that kept it.
Not recoverable, with the reason Git has

151
00:10:36,411 --> 00:10:41,067
no copy. Check that your list has the
second column, and that your message to the

152
00:10:41,107 --> 00:10:42,575
colleague says it plainly.

153
00:10:42,915 --> 00:10:47,915
Stage 6, a deleted branch. Primary for
GitHub knowledge and debugging ability.

154
00:10:48,255 --> 00:10:52,628
The model looks at what each clone still
has, and deliberately doesn't fetch with

155
00:10:52,668 --> 00:10:55,842
pruning: that would delete the
one copy of the name it has.

156
00:10:55,882 --> 00:10:59,983
Its own remote-tracking branch, its
record of the server's branch, shows two

157
00:11:00,023 --> 00:11:02,053
commits. The tech lead had seen three.

158
00:11:02,393 --> 00:11:07,441
The pull request ref holds a third commit,
d3ed8df, that no clone's branch had.

159
00:11:07,481 --> 00:11:09,373
The model restores from there.

160
00:11:09,713 --> 00:11:14,169
You may have found the tip elsewhere, and
that scores the same if it is evidenced.

161
00:11:14,209 --> 00:11:18,665
What the deliverable asks is the list of
copies that existed, per repository, each

162
00:11:18,705 --> 00:11:22,777
with its commit, which one you used and
why, and the GitHub instrument that

163
00:11:22,817 --> 00:11:25,244
corresponds to your
recovery, with its limit.

164
00:11:25,584 --> 00:11:31,022
Stage 7, a broken pull request. Primary
for Git knowledge and recovery skills.

165
00:11:31,062 --> 00:11:36,219
This is the senior standard of video
188 with three people instead of two.

166
00:11:36,559 --> 00:11:41,516
Before publishing, the model proves two
things. Every replayed commit pairs with its

167
00:11:41,556 --> 00:11:46,395
original under git range-diff, which compares
two versions of a series of commits.

168
00:11:46,435 --> 00:11:51,095
And the tree of the rebuilt branch equals
the tree that a merge of the server's

169
00:11:51,135 --> 00:11:55,616
state and your own work would give: two
equal IDs, and the tests pass on it.

170
00:11:55,956 --> 00:12:00,176
In your stage file: is there one
line for each of the eleven steps?

171
00:12:00,216 --> 00:12:04,563
Is the proof that the repair changed
no content made before the push?

172
00:12:04,603 --> 00:12:09,141
And does your forced push name what
it expects? A bare --force here is a

173
00:12:09,181 --> 00:12:10,540
disqualifying finding.

174
00:12:10,880 --> 00:12:16,009
Stage 8, a hotfix and backport. Primary
for production judgment and engineering

175
00:12:16,049 --> 00:12:21,113
communication. The proposal in the briefing
was to merge the fix and tag main.

176
00:12:21,453 --> 00:12:26,401
What would that release? Fifteen files
differ between the last release and main,

177
00:12:26,441 --> 00:12:31,513
seven of them in the router package. A
patch release tagged on main would ship all

178
00:12:31,553 --> 00:12:34,817
of it. That's video 172 as
a decision under pressure.

179
00:12:35,157 --> 00:12:39,642
The backport, the copy of the fix on the
maintenance line, next to the original.

180
00:12:39,682 --> 00:12:44,280
The port needed adaptation to the older
code, so by default the comparison doesn't

181
00:12:44,320 --> 00:12:48,861
pair the two commits at all. Made to pair
them, it shows what was adapted and the

182
00:12:48,901 --> 00:12:50,332
"cherry picked from" line.

183
00:12:50,672 --> 00:12:55,511
The evaluation's level 4 for Git knowledge
names this: the write-up predicts why a

184
00:12:55,551 --> 00:13:00,092
patch comparison misreports the backport.
Check your stage file for the three

185
00:13:00,132 --> 00:13:04,614
answers it asks. What exactly the patch
release contains, as a diff summary.

186
00:13:04,654 --> 00:13:09,612
Whether it's a regression. And the command
by which someone verifies next month that

187
00:13:09,652 --> 00:13:10,743
the fix is on main.

188
00:13:11,083 --> 00:13:14,345
Each check fails before its
solution and passes after it.

189
00:13:14,385 --> 00:13:18,805
That's the entry condition, eight times.
And it answers the question from the

190
00:13:18,845 --> 00:13:21,875
opening. Eight passing checks
don't mean you're done.

191
00:13:21,915 --> 00:13:24,076
They mean your work may now be scored.

192
00:13:24,416 --> 00:13:29,892
The first-parent history of main after the
fortnight, the line you get by following

193
00:13:29,932 --> 00:13:35,408
only the first parent of each merge: every
line is a pull request, squash or merge,

194
00:13:35,448 --> 00:13:40,924
and nothing was pushed to it directly. The
maintenance line release/1.3 carries one

195
00:13:40,964 --> 00:13:46,175
commit, tagged v1.3.2. Compare with your
own sandbox: git log --first-parent on

196
00:13:46,215 --> 00:13:51,558
main. Your IDs differ for the commits you
made. The shape shouldn't surprise you.

197
00:13:54,898 --> 00:13:58,406
Now complete the sheet: medians,
rounded down, and floors.

198
00:13:58,446 --> 00:14:02,014
Stop the video while you do,
and say your outcome out loud.

199
00:14:06,854 --> 00:14:11,027
Then answer three questions in writing.
In which stage was your evidence log

200
00:14:11,067 --> 00:14:15,627
thinnest, and what would you collect now?
Which control should each stage have left

201
00:14:15,667 --> 00:14:20,062
behind, and on which layer? And what would
you do differently in a real incident

202
00:14:20,102 --> 00:14:20,561
tomorrow?

203
00:14:22,401 --> 00:14:24,049
Five mistakes to watch for.

204
00:14:24,389 --> 00:14:29,264
Scoring from memory. Root cause: you remember
what you understood, and the evaluator

205
00:14:29,304 --> 00:14:31,312
can score only what the page shows.

206
00:14:31,652 --> 00:14:36,656
Comparing commands instead of safety. Root
cause: the model solution is one route,

207
00:14:36,696 --> 00:14:41,268
and a different route that is evidenced,
safe and verified scores the same.

208
00:14:41,608 --> 00:14:47,086
Treating PASS as a score. Root cause: the
check verifies Git state only; hypotheses,

209
00:14:47,126 --> 00:14:51,816
options, leases, messages and what
was not verified are invisible to it.

210
00:14:52,156 --> 00:14:56,833
Hiding your own error. Root cause: a
mistake you report and repair in your own

211
00:14:56,873 --> 00:15:01,792
deliverable scores level 2 for that stage;
the same mistake found by the evaluator

212
00:15:01,832 --> 00:15:02,699
can disqualify.

213
00:15:03,039 --> 00:15:07,909
Averaging a bad stage away. Root cause:
the floor is the lowest level among a

214
00:15:07,949 --> 00:15:13,202
dimension's primary stages, so one primary
stage below level 3 decides the outcome.

215
00:15:15,042 --> 00:15:19,526
Now, out of the lab. A learner scores
her capstone a day after finishing.

216
00:15:19,566 --> 00:15:24,361
Her checks all pass. Her first pass over
the sheet gives her threes and fours.

217
00:15:24,701 --> 00:15:28,001
Then she applies the quickest
read to her own files.

218
00:15:28,041 --> 00:15:33,076
In stage 4 her "Not verified" list is
empty. In stage 6 she has one hypothesis,

219
00:15:33,116 --> 00:15:37,573
confirmed. In stage 7 her forced push
is written with a lease and with

220
00:15:37,613 --> 00:15:42,840
--force-if-includes, on a branch she had
examined a moment before, which the rules

221
00:15:42,880 --> 00:15:44,446
of the simulation accept.

222
00:15:44,786 --> 00:15:49,781
But the proof that the repair changed no
content comes after the push in her log,

223
00:15:49,821 --> 00:15:55,002
not before. She corrects three scores
downward. Debugging ability now has a floor of

224
00:15:55,042 --> 00:15:57,800
2, because stage 6 is a
primary stage for it.

225
00:15:58,140 --> 00:16:01,446
The outcome on her own sheet
is "not yet", with a list.

226
00:16:01,486 --> 00:16:06,557
Debugging ability, stage 6. Restudy the
method chapter and the history-investigation

227
00:16:06,597 --> 00:16:10,268
sections the evaluation names.
Two incidents she hasn't done.

228
00:16:10,308 --> 00:16:14,223
Then retake the stage in a rebuilt
sandbox with new deliverables.

229
00:16:14,263 --> 00:16:19,273
She does that in a week. Her note on the
retake says what changed: three hypotheses

230
00:16:19,313 --> 00:16:24,141
were on the page before the first test,
and one of them, the one she had thought

231
00:16:24,181 --> 00:16:25,601
unlikely, was the cause.

232
00:16:27,441 --> 00:16:32,928
Your turn. Fill in the scoring sheet of
capstone/EVALUATION.md for your own work,

233
00:16:32,968 --> 00:16:37,499
stage by stage, before you watch
each stage's replay in this video.

234
00:16:37,839 --> 00:16:41,678
For each cell, predict the level
before you open your stage file.

235
00:16:41,718 --> 00:16:45,258
Then open the file and point at
the evidence for that level.

236
00:16:45,298 --> 00:16:50,210
If you can't point at it, lower the level.
After each replay, write one line: where

237
00:16:50,250 --> 00:16:54,984
your path differed from the model's, and
whether your path was as safe, with the

238
00:16:55,024 --> 00:16:59,578
reason. Compute medians and floors, and
apply the six conditions of the final

239
00:16:59,618 --> 00:17:00,115
judgment.

240
00:17:00,455 --> 00:17:05,793
The challenge: rewrite the weakest of
your eight CTO messages so that it meets

241
00:17:05,833 --> 00:17:10,687
section 30.15, and the final postmortem
so that it meets section 30.19.

242
00:17:10,727 --> 00:17:13,789
Apply the test: replace
every name by a role.

243
00:17:15,629 --> 00:17:18,330
Read the question, then answer
out loud, in two minutes.

244
00:17:23,170 --> 00:17:28,008
The question has a fixed form: three defaults,
and for each a layer and a decision.

245
00:17:28,048 --> 00:17:32,827
A strong answer picks three from different
layers, so that the answer covers a Git

246
00:17:32,867 --> 00:17:37,588
client default, a platform default and a
pipeline default, and names each default

247
00:17:37,628 --> 00:17:42,466
precisely. For each it says why the default
is reasonable in general, which is what

248
00:17:42,506 --> 00:17:45,757
makes it a default, and what
went wrong in the incident.

249
00:17:46,097 --> 00:17:50,614
Then the decision, with its reason: where
changing the default is enough, where it's

250
00:17:50,654 --> 00:17:55,170
too weak because it lives in one clone, and
where the right answer is a control on a

251
00:17:55,210 --> 00:17:57,828
stronger layer. End on the
limit of that control.

252
00:17:59,668 --> 00:18:00,595
Let's land this.

253
00:18:00,935 --> 00:18:05,850
The capstone is judged from three sources:
the eight checks, the stage files and the

254
00:18:05,890 --> 00:18:09,566
postmortem; a passing check is
an entry condition, not a score.

255
00:18:09,906 --> 00:18:15,050
Each dimension is scored per stage on four
levels; level 3 means correct, evidenced

256
00:18:15,090 --> 00:18:19,984
and safe, and level 4 adds weighed
alternatives, stated limits, the layer and a

257
00:18:20,024 --> 00:18:20,483
control.

258
00:18:20,823 --> 00:18:25,787
The outcome needs every dimension score
and every floor at 3 or higher, at least

259
00:18:25,827 --> 00:18:30,791
three dimensions at 4 including production
judgment or debugging ability, and no

260
00:18:30,831 --> 00:18:32,167
disqualifying finding.

261
00:18:32,507 --> 00:18:37,384
A different route that is evidenced, safe
and verified scores the same as the model

262
00:18:37,424 --> 00:18:37,740
route.

263
00:18:38,080 --> 00:18:42,121
You score from the page: what is not
written down was not demonstrated.

264
00:18:43,961 --> 00:18:48,881
Enter what the capstone showed in the
weak-area tracker of section 12 of the

265
00:18:48,921 --> 00:18:54,102
roadmap, curriculum/Git and GitHub mastery
roadmap.md, and plan the restudy with

266
00:18:54,142 --> 00:18:59,454
revision/revision-checklist.md. If your
own sheet says "not yet", take the restudy

267
00:18:59,494 --> 00:19:04,805
the evaluation names for the dimension that
fell short, and retake that stage in a

268
00:19:04,845 --> 00:19:10,287
rebuilt sandbox. Part 11, the last part,
looks ahead: where Git is going, and how to

269
00:19:10,327 --> 00:19:13,028
read its primary sources without a course.

270
00:19:13,368 --> 00:19:18,183
Today you read your own work the way a
reviewer reads it, and that's a skill few

271
00:19:18,223 --> 00:19:23,160
engineers ever practise. Whatever your
sheet says, you now know exactly what to do

272
00:19:23,200 --> 00:19:27,832
next. Next time: the road to Git 3.0,
with its planned defaults and removals.

273
00:19:27,872 --> 00:19:30,988
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

274
00:19:31,028 --> 00:19:32,445
See you in the next one.
